>
  <p class="description scribble-target">Language models are not reliable in the way that calculators are reliable. They are probabilistic systems that produce plausible outputs — which is useful, but requires a calibrated approach to verification.</p>

  <h2 class="note-heading">The Trust Spectrum</h2>
  <p class="note-text">Trust in model output should be proportional to the cost of being wrong, the verifiability of the output, and the model's known performance profile on similar tasks. There is no binary "trust / don't trust" — there is a spectrum, and responsible deployment means placing each use case at the right point on it.</p>
  <ul class="note-list">
    <li><strong>High trust, low review</strong>: formatting, style edits, translation of well-understood text, summarisation of content you already understand. Errors are low-stakes and quickly caught.</li>
    <li><strong>Medium trust, spot review</strong>: first drafts, research synthesis, code generation in non-critical paths. Sample the output systematically rather than reviewing everything.</li>
    <li><strong>Low trust, full review</strong>: legal or compliance text, medical information, financial calculations, any claim that will be published as fact. Treat model output as a starting point, not a conclusion.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="note-heading">Where Models Reliably Fail</h2>
  <ul class="note-list">
    <li><strong>Recency</strong> — models have a training cutoff. Events, pricing, regulatory changes, and market conditions after that date are unavailable to the model and will be fabricated with varying confidence.</li>
    <li><strong>Precise citation</strong> — models confabulate references. Any citation in model output must be independently verified before use. This includes URLs, journal articles, and quoted statistics.</li>
    <li><strong>Mathematical precision</strong> — standard language models are not calculators. Multi-step arithmetic errors are common. Use code execution or tool-augmented models for numerical work.</li>
    <li><strong>Self-knowledge</strong> — models are unreliable reporters of their own capabilities and limitations. "I don't know" is often not in the training distribution for confident-sounding output.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="note-heading">Building a Verification Habit</h2>
  <ul class="note-list">
    <li>Define review requirements before deployment, not after an error. For each AI-assisted workflow, specify what gets reviewed, by whom, and at what frequency.</li>
    <li>Create a short checklist of failure modes specific to your domain. A marketing team's checklist looks different from a legal team's — tailor it to what matters.</li>
    <li>Track error rates over time. As you use a model on a specific task category, you will develop a calibrated sense of where it fails. This is institutional knowledge worth documenting.</li>
    <li>Distinguish between errors that are caught before delivery and errors that reach the end user. The latter are disproportionately costly to reputation and trust.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="note-heading">A Note on Confidence</h2>
  <p class="note-text">Language models express uncertainty poorly. A model that is wrong about something often sounds identical to a model that is right. This is not a bug to be fixed — it is a structural property of how these systems generate text. The implication is that fluency and confidence in model output are not signals of accuracy. Build processes that do not rely on the model flagging its own uncertainty.</p>
</NoteLayout>
